; micro-PROLOG resurrection source — v025 second-review improvement baseline
; Original disassembly generated by z80dasm 1.1.3
; command line: z80dasm -a -t -l -g 24576 -b blocks.txt prolog.bin

; ROM routines

; ============================================================================
; CHAPTER 0 — THE MACHINE THAT THE REST OF THE SOURCE ASSUMES
; ============================================================================
;
; A disassembler sees addresses and instructions.  The interpreter sees a much
; smaller, tidier machine of its own invention: three-byte terms, six-byte heap
; objects, twelve-byte execution records, and a handful of fixed workspace
; words which act like registers for that virtual machine.
;
; This file gives names to that invented machine.  It emits no bytes.  Reading
; it first is worthwhile because nearly every short sequence later in the
; source is manipulating one of the layouts declared here.  In particular,
; remember these three scales:
;
;       3 bytes   one tagged term cell
;       6 bytes   one managed heap object or trail node
;      12 bytes   one execution frame / choice-point snapshot
;
; The Z80 registers are only temporary couriers.  The durable interpreter state
; lives in the fixed words beginning at 0x9800, named below.
;
; The interpreter deliberately delegates hardware-facing work to the Spectrum
; ROM.  ROM routines are treated as an imported machine substrate: keyboard
; scanning, screen channels, cursor handling, graphics primitives, sound, and
; cassette pulse timing remain ROM responsibilities.  Everything above that
; boundary--terms, clauses, unification, modules, and the supervisor--belongs to
; micro-PROLOG itself.  A reader can therefore treat the ROM symbols below like
; an operating-system API rather than unexplained pieces of the interpreter.
ROM_LD_EDGE_1:                 equ 005e7h
ROM_LD_EDGE_2:                 equ 005e3h
ROM_OUT_CURS_WITHOUT_CHECK:    equ 018e8h
ROM_INPUT_AD:                  equ 015e6h
ROM_PRINT_A_2:                 equ 015f2h

